    (
        6,
        4,
        "",
        "Hear, Israel",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Sh’ma Yisra’el (<em>שְׁמַע</em> <em>יִשְׂרָאֵל</em>).</strong> The most famous verse in Judaism — recited twice daily in the morning and evening prayers, taught to children, said as the last words of the dying. Its translation is famously contested: “Yahweh our God, Yahweh is one” / “Yahweh is our God, Yahweh alone” / “Yahweh our God is one Yahweh.” Each is grammatically defensible; each carries a different theological emphasis (oneness vs. exclusivity vs. unity). Jesus quotes this verse as the first of the two greatest commandments (Mark 12:29).",
        "User original",
    ),
