        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy might&#x27; (Deu 6:5), Ephrem reads the threefold-totality of love as the canonical Old-Testament anchor of the Great Commandment that Christ cites as &#x27;the first and great commandment&#x27; (Mt 22:37-38). Heart, soul, might: nothing of the human person is exempt from the demand. Tewahedo monastic theology cites Deu 6:5 + Mt 22:37 as the canonical doubled-witness that monastic poverty (yielding might), monastic chastity (yielding heart), and monastic obedience (yielding soul) are the threefold practical realization of the one Shema-commandment.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 6:4-9. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Mt 22:37-38.",
