        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (365).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Deuteronomy</em> <small>(c. 365 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;The Lord thy God will raise up unto thee a Prophet from the midst of thee, of thy brethren, like unto me; unto him ye shall hearken&#x27; (Deu 18:15), Ephrem reads the prophet-like-Moses promise as the canonical Old-Testament messianic anchor cited verbatim by Peter in Acts 3:22 (&#x27;A prophet shall the Lord your God raise up unto you of your brethren, like unto me&#x27;) and by Stephen in Acts 7:37 — the apostolic preaching&#x27;s primary Old-Testament prophecy-of-Christ proof-text alongside Ps 110:1. The &#x27;like-Moses&#x27; qualification is fulfilled in Christ at the Transfiguration where Moses and Elijah appear with him on the mountain. Tewahedo christology cites Deu 18:15 + Acts 3:22 + the Buhe Transfiguration as the canonical triple-witness of the Mosaic-prophet identity of Christ.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Deuteronomy, on 18:9-22. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13. PD. Cf. Acts 3:22 (verbatim apostolic citation), Acts 7:37.",
