    (
        23,
        3,
        "",
        "An Ammonite or a  Moabite",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The exclusion clause.</strong> Read straightforwardly, this verse permanently bars Moabites from the assembly of Yahweh — yet the Book of Ruth tells the story of a Moabite woman who becomes the great-grandmother of King David. Already within the Hebrew Bible, the rule and its great exception sit side by side. The rabbis later distinguished “Moabite (man)” from “Moabite woman,” preserving Ruth’s standing. The passage became a flashpoint in post-exilic readings (Nehemiah 13:1–3) and a model of how the canon contains its own internal critiques.",
        "User original",
    ),
