    (
        32,
        8,
        "",
        "children of Israel",
        "source",
        "Variant",
        "Variant",
        "<strong>“Sons of God” or “sons of Israel”?</strong> One of the most famous textual variants in the Hebrew Bible. The Masoretic Text reads <em>b’nei Yisra’el</em> (“sons of Israel”) — followed by the WEB here. The Dead Sea Scrolls (4QDeut<sup>j</sup>), the Septuagint, and the Old Latin read <em>b’nei elohim</em> (“sons of God”) or <em>angeloi theou</em> (“angels of God”). The variant matters theologically: the older reading depicts God allotting nations to subordinate divine beings, while keeping Israel for himself (32:9). This more polytheistic-sounding text was likely smoothed by Masoretic transmission. Many modern translations (ESV, NRSV, NABRE) restore the older reading.",
        "User paraphrase; references Septuagint, Old Latin",
    ),
