    (
        33,
        2,
        "",
        "Yahweh came from Sinai",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The blessing of Moses.</strong> Chapter 33 — Moses’ final blessing on the tribes — opens with one of the oldest Hebrew theophany texts. The Sinai-to-Seir-to-Paran progression maps onto the southern desert geography from which Israelite tradition pictures Yahweh emerging. Habakkuk 3:3 echoes the same itinerary. The phrase “fiery law” (or “fire of law,” <em>esh dat</em>) at the end of v. 2 is textually difficult and probably represents a corruption — the LXX and several manuscripts have a different reading altogether. A reminder of how much of biblical poetry sits at the edge of recoverable text.",
        "User paraphrase; references LXX",
    ),
