        27,
        "",
        "Underneath are the everlasting arms",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>"The everlasting arms."</strong> Moses’ blessing of the tribes ends with one of the OT’s most enduring poetic formulas: the eternal God as dwelling-place (<em>m‘onah</em>, "refuge"), with everlasting arms beneath. The image inverts what one would expect — divine arms typically come from above (the "outstretched arm" of the exodus, Exod 6:6) — to position Yahweh as the bearer beneath, the foundational support that catches and holds. The phrase has had perhaps the longest devotional afterlife of any single Deuteronomic verse in English-language Christianity through the gospel hymn "Leaning on the Everlasting Arms" (E. A. Hoffman, 1887), which carries the verse’s exact formulation into popular religion. The blessing-poem of Deut 33 as a whole — the parallel to Jacob’s deathbed blessing in Gen 49 — is one of the oldest Hebrew compositions in the Pentateuch, preserved with archaic language and difficult lexical items, and is widely treated as preserving genuine pre-monarchic poetic tradition.',
