        "what god is there in heaven or in earth that can do works like yours",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The rhetorical-monolatrous question.</strong> Moses’ prayer poses the question — what god in heaven or earth can do such works as Yahweh? — in a form that does not categorically deny other gods’ existence but asserts Yahweh’s incomparable power among them. The construction is characteristic of Israelite monolatrous theology in its earlier strata: the existence of the surrounding pantheon is acknowledged or left untouched, while Yahweh’s practical supremacy is asserted (compare Exod 15:11, "Who is like you, Yahweh, among the gods?"; Ps 86:8, 89:6). The fully developed monotheistic claim — that other gods do not exist at all — emerges most clearly in Second Isaiah (Isa 44:6, 45:5–6, 18). The verse is one of many that show the OT theological development from monolatry (one Yahweh among real gods) toward strict monotheism (Yahweh alone is real) as a long process internal to the canon, not a single moment.',
        "User original",
